{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790329554,"nonce":"fxbbhlgCfWw4qdY8FRmqFWMOK+Y=","title":"CPython C API for zypy as a ZRTL plugin linked into the executable","message":"zypy must load and run stock CPython extension modules (cp3XY wheels for every maintained 3.x that pip serves, 3.9 to 3.14 today, plus abi3) without a libpython: numpy first, and the hand-written C, Cython, pybind11 and PyO3 modules around it. The census behind this (295 extension modules from 12 packages, symbol unions, layouts measured with offsetof against the 3.9 to 3.15rc2 headers) is in the cpyapi scratch work; its numbers are quoted where a decision rests on them.\n\nDecision. The C API is a ZRTL plugin crate owned by the Python frontend, crates/zyntax_python/capi (package zyntax_python_capi, plugin name \"python_capi\", ZRTL symbols `$CPy$*`), statically linked into zypy with `linked-into-host`, never a dylib. It is the same split as the Lua C API (c17ca09391383182a1cf10565b3676b5eddfa2325184d78ecc9e30515476f489) and reuses its generic Zyntax pieces. zypy keeps every representation it has (typed scalars, typed lists, shaped tuples, ZRTL strings, DynamicBox Any, instance structs). A value gets a CPython-layout object only when it crosses into C, and a C object is held on the zypy side by a FOREIGN box. Everything that differs between CPython minor versions is one row of a table inside the plugin, chosen once per process from the extension tags. Compiled zypy code never reads a CPython layout, so a row is runtime data: adding a CPython release adds a row and never touches the snapshot.\n\nThree designs were weighed. The cpyext-style mirror and the staged-by-ABI-surface design agree on the model above and are grafted together. Layout-compatible boxing (make Python's Any a real PyObject in the row's layout) is rejected: it moves every dynamic path in zyntax_builtins, the list header the compiler recognises, instance layout (+8 bytes per object), the hash definitions and the pending-exception global onto CPython layouts before any extension loads, so every pure program pays and the performance record is at risk; it also turns row offsets into link constants in the snapshot, so a row stops being data. Its hash-agreement finding is kept (see Objects).\n\n\nCrates and ownership\n\n1. sdk/zrtl_native (from c17ca09391383182a1cf10565b3676b5eddfa2325184d78ecc9e30515476f489, language-neutral, not built for wasm32). Python uses `Library::open(path, Scope::Local)` (dlopen RTLD_NOW|RTLD_LOCAL, as CPython does; extensions still bind to the executable's exports through the flat namespace on macOS and the global scope on Linux), `symbol(name)`, the dlerror text, and the build helper `export_link_args(target, names)` (exported_symbols_list with `_main` on macOS, `--dynamic-list` on Linux, .def on Windows). Python does not use boundary.c: its errors are return codes and no C frame is ever unwound. The compiler test that an indirect call with CallingConvention::Default reaches an `extern \"C\"` target with pointer, i64 and f64 arguments on the interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM is shared; Python's later inline caches rest on it too.\n\n2. Collector hooks: add_weak_hooks (hold, trace to a fixpoint, settle), add_weak_sweeper and add_after_collection, written for Lua's weak tables in 842e068179a8e9fcc0f0f9a324abddae36b6bfe6 on w2/L-gc-semantics and not yet on main. They cover everything the C API needs until phase 6: `trace` roots zypy values that C holds, a sweeper drops dead table entries before the sweep, and after-collection releases C references (tp_dealloc may run program code). Phase 6 adds one generic piece Lua does not need yet: a mutator handoff (the holder of an embedder-declared lock is the mutator; a thread that released it parks its stack window as a root range) so that threads other than the main one may allocate.\n\n3. crates/zyntax_builtins: FOREIGN_TAG, the next custom kind after FILE_TAG, meaning \"an object owned by a foreign runtime\", and extern hooks zb_hook_foreign_{call,callmethod,getattr,setattr,delattr,getitem,setitem,delitem,arith,unary,compare,hash,len,bool,str,repr,iter,next,contains,isinstance} behind a `Policy.foreign_hooks` flag. The arms sit after the category, instance and shape tests, on paths that raise TypeError today, so no existing path gains an instruction. Nothing in it names Python.\n\n4. crates/zyntax_python/capi (Python semantics). Depends on zrtl, zrtl_native and zyntax_builtins' tag constants; frontend hooks arrive through `install(FrontendHooks)` after compile, as function pointers from the runtime (py$exc take and set, zb_call_N, keyword-aware call, py$getattr/setattr by interned name, module table access, exception class map, runtime import entry).\n   - symbols.toml: one entry per name (function or data, first and last minor version, alias target for renames, stable-ABI flag taken from Misc/stable_abi.toml). build.rs generates EXPORTS, the export list and the Rust alias table from it. The exported set is the union over rows, 558 names for 3.9 to 3.14 GIL (513 for cp314). Renames alias one implementation (_PyThreadState_UncheckedGet and PyThreadState_GetUnchecked, _Py_HashBytes and Py_HashBuffer, _PyFloat_Pack8 and PyFloat_Pack8, the _SizeT names).\n   - Every name is exported from phase 1. A name not implemented yet is a veneer stub that sets SystemError(\"PyXxx is not provided by zypy yet\") and returns the API's error value; a stub with no error channel aborts with the same message. Data symbols are always real. So dlopen never fails on a missing name, and `ZYPY_CAPI_TRACE=1` lists, at load, which of a module's undefined Py* symbols are still stubs (read from its Mach-O or ELF symbol table).\n   - c/varargs.c, compiled with cc: the C-variadic entry points (PyArg_ParseTuple, ParseTupleAndKeywords, UnpackTuple, VaParse*, Py_BuildValue, Py_VaBuildValue, PyErr_Format, PyErr_WarnFormat, PyUnicode_FromFormat(V), PyObject_CallFunction(ObjArgs), PyObject_CallMethod(ObjArgs), PyTuple_Pack, PyOS_snprintf and the _SizeT aliases). Each walks its format with va_arg into a slot array and calls a non-variadic Rust core. Stable Rust cannot define C-variadic functions. Written fresh; the archive links whole-archive on Linux, and static_plugin() reads EXPORTS through black_box so no linker drops a member (the Lua rule).\n   - src/abi/rows.rs and select.rs (the table and the process row), obj/ (pre-header allocator, immortals, MIRRORS and PROXIES tables, freelists), types/ (one file per builtin, typeobject.rs with PyType_Ready, FromSpec, FromMetaclass, slot inheritance, _PyType_Lookup with a version-tag cache), boundary/ (to_c, from_c, list and dict view sync, trampolines), errors.rs, tstate.rs, gil.rs, import.rs (loader), args.rs (PyArg and BuildValue cores), buffer.rs, capsule.rs, datetime.rs.\n\n5. crates/zyntax_python (frontend): the runtime import system (a prerequisite, below) with the extension finder; FOREIGN module values; the FOREIGN arms in the dispatchers it generates (py$call$m$n, py$getattr$x, except matching, isinstance), emitted only when the program's import closure can reach an extension or imports dynamically; per-class py$getattr$dyn and py$setattr$dyn by interned name; a per-module table (name to global slot, boxing reader and writer) for module objects C reads; parameter names and defaults on function records for keyword calls from C; foreign-based classes; a nullable `$cpy` field on the BaseException prelude class; build.rs passes EXPORTS to export_link_args in place of the `_main`-only emit_link_flags (the same function exists in zyntax_lua and zynml build.rs and moves to the helper with them).\n\n\nVersions\n\nOne row per process. Symbols bind globally by name and objects flow between modules (numpy tuples reach numpy.random's Cython, orjson serialises numpy scalars), so one tuple cannot carry ob_item at 24 for a 3.13 module and at 32 for a 3.14 one. CPython enforces the same rule. The row is chosen by the first of: the tags of the extensions the program imports statically (resolved at compile time, so a mix is a compile error naming each file and tag); a venv's pyvenv.cfg version or the python3.XY in its site-packages path; the first versioned extension loaded at run time. An abi3 module loads under any row whose minor is at least its cp3X (from the dist-info WHEEL tag when present); with only abi3 modules the newest row (3.14) is used. A later module with another cpython tag raises ImportError naming both tags. A cp3XYt file is refused with \"free-threaded CPython builds are not supported yet\" until phase 7.\n\nA row (src/abi/rows.rs) holds: tag, Py_Version hexversion (0x030E07F0 for 3.14.7), EXTENSION_SUFFIXES per OS in CPython's order; sizeof PyTypeObject (408 for 3.9 to 3.11, 416 for 3.12 to 3.14, 424 for 3.15), PyHeapTypeObject size and the offsets of as_async, as_number, as_mapping, as_sequence, as_buffer, ht_name, ht_qualname, ht_module (880 to 936 over 3.9 to 3.14), PyAsyncMethods size (24 on 3.9, 32 after); tuple ob_item (24, or 32 with ob_hash on 3.14); LongLayout (SignInSize for 3.9 to 3.11, LvTag after); unicode header sizes (PyASCIIObject 48 and compact 72 with wstr for 3.9 to 3.11, 40 and 56 after), bit 7 meaning (ready, or statically_allocated); dict version tag offset where Cython reads it (3.9 to 3.11); PyThreadState size (264 to 832), interp, the error slot (ErrorSlot::CurExc triple at 88 or 96, ErrorSlot::CurrentException at 96, 112 or 136), exc_info (144, 144, 120, 104, 120, 120), the exc item shape (triple on 3.9 and 3.10), gilstate_counter (160, 160, 136, 120, 136, 136); PyBaseExceptionObject traceback (32 or 40); the frame fields Cython writes inline; whether tp_watched and tp_versions_used exist (PyType_Ready never writes past the row's type size, which with one row per process is always the module's own compiled size). Symbol availability comes from symbols.toml, not the row.\n\nWhat is not in the row: refcounting. Runtime-owned objects (static types, the PyExc_* types, None, True, False, NotImplemented, Ellipsis, small ints -5 to 256, empty tuple, str and bytes, interned constants) carry ob_refcnt low word 0xC0000000 with the high word 0 and ob_flags clear. That is 3.14's own immortal initial value and immortal under the 3.12 and 3.13 rules; 3.9 to 3.11 code and abi3 built below limited 3.12 move it by one per ++/-- and never reach 0. Verified by running the stock markupsafe _speedups for cp314, cp313, cp312, cp311 and cp39 (macOS arm64, and Linux x86_64 on the NUC) against scratch hosts that change only the row.\n\nA test in the capi crate compiles an offsetof program against each vendored Include tree and fails on any row field that differs, so adding 3.15 is its headers, its row and a green test. The 3.15 offsets are already measured.\n\nLatest stable: python.org's release API lists 3.14.7 (2026-08-05) as the newest release; 3.15 is at rc2. References: uv's cpython-3.14.7-macos-aarch64-none on the Mac (pyenv holds only 3.11.9 and 2.7.18) with a venv holding numpy 2.5.3 cp314 that imports, and python3 3.14.4 on the NUC. Older rows are proved against uv's CPython of that minor version.\n\n\nObjects\n\nThe boundary has two tables. MIRRORS maps a zypy value (its storage address; the collector does not move objects) to the CPython-layout object C sees. PROXIES maps a C object to the one FOREIGN box zypy holds for it, so `is` stays pointer equality on both sides while the objects live.\n\nEvery object the plugin allocates (its own constructors, PyType_GenericAlloc, _PyObject_New/NewVar, PyObject_GC_New/NewVar) is malloc'd with a 16-byte pre-header {link, gc}. link is the zypy value for a mirror and 0 otherwise; gc holds the tracked bit and, from phase 6, traversal state. C never reads it. These objects are outside the pool: the collector neither scans nor frees them. Objects a C type allocates itself carry no link. PyObject_Malloc and PyMem_* are malloc.\n\nzypy to C, to_c(v), returns a new reference:\n- None, bool, NotImplemented, Ellipsis: the static singletons.\n- int: the immortal small-int table, else a PyLongObject in the row's encoding (lv_tag = ndigits\u003c\u003c3 | sign with 30-bit digits at 24 from 3.12; sign in ob_size before) from a freelist.\n- float: a PyFloatObject (ob_fval at 16) from a freelist. complex likewise.\n- str: a compact PyUnicodeObject at the row's layout, kind the narrowest that fits, cached in MIRRORS by the ZRTL string's storage, so a string crosses in O(len) once. ASCII is one memcpy (known in O(1) from the ZRTL header). For non-ASCII, utf8 and utf8_length point at the ZRTL string's bytes (the mirror's link keeps them alive), which needs the terminated-string flag the Lua design adds to sdk/zrtl, or a copy until it lands. Exact str stays compact because CPython's exact str always is.\n- bytes: a PyBytesObject (ob_shash -1, ob_sval at 32, NUL), mirrored. bytearray: a PyByteArrayObject whose ob_start and ob_bytes point at the zypy buffer.\n- tuple: a boxed tuple gets a mirror with ob_item at the row's offset (and ob_hash -1 on 3.14); a shaped value tuple has no identity in zypy and becomes a fresh PyTuple each crossing.\n- list, dict, set, frozenset, class instances, function values, bound methods, classes, modules: the value's mirror (below).\n- a FOREIGN box: its PyObject*.\nArguments handed to C calls are new references released after the call, so C never sees a zypy value at refcount 1 inside a call. Mirrors start at 1, the table's own reference.\n\nC to zypy, from_c(o):\n- A pre-header link gives back the original zypy value (markupsafe returning its argument yields the same object; checked in the scratch hosts).\n- Exact int within i64 becomes an int; wider stays a proxy until arbitrary-precision int (d692f0a354b274e582b56404f376a4777693f629e73fee7f3a100e3b83c19c02) lands, arithmetic through PyNumber. Exact float becomes f64, bool becomes bool.\n- Exact str, bytes, tuple are converted once and paired (the C object's link and a MIRRORS entry), so later crossings are O(1).\n- An exact list or dict C created and handed over at refcount 1 is adopted as a zypy container and the C object freed; otherwise it stays a proxy.\n- Everything else is a proxy: ndarray, dtype, numpy scalars (np.float64 is a PyFloat subtype, so only exact types convert), C modules, C types, C functions.\n\nLists are the one type with two storages, because PyList_GET_ITEM, SET_ITEM and Cython's in-place append read and write ob_item inline and zypy's list holds boxes, not PyObject*. A list mirror's ob_item is filled when the list crosses and written back when control returns (changed length or item pointers are converted back). While C retains the mirror (refcount above 1 after the call), it is refreshed at every zypy-to-C transition and written back at every return. A C write that does not fit a typed list's element type raises TypeError naming both types; the list is never retyped. dict and set are live views: PyDictObject is {header, ma_used at 16, the row's version tag, a private pointer to the zypy dict}, every PyDict_* and PySet_* works on the zypy container, and ma_used of retained views is refreshed at each transition (O(1) each). Borrowed results (PyDict_GetItem*, PyDict_Next) come from a per-view side array of value mirrors, valid until overwritten or until the view dies.\n\nProxies hold exactly one reference, however many zypy variables alias the box, and C calls borrow it (CPython 3.14 locals are borrowed too). So PyUnstable_Object_IsUniquelyReferenced is refcount == 1 and numpy's ndarray.resize works as on 3.14 (numpy shape.c refuses any array that is not uniquely referenced; a design that counted zypy's hold as two references would refuse every resize). PyUnstable_Object_IsUniqueReferencedTemporary answers 1 only for an argument the call site marks as a dying temporary (phase 5, from drop analysis: the box is dead after the call); until then it answers 0 and numpy's in-place elision stays off, which is safe. Before 3.14 numpy's elision needs a backtrace that reaches `_PyEval_EvalFrameDefault` through libpython frames; zypy's frames never match (JIT addresses fail dladdr), and the plugin must never export that name. Known difference: two zypy aliases of one ndarray still count as one, so resize succeeds where CPython refuses.\n\nLiveness. A trace hook marks the zypy value of every mirror whose refcount exceeds the table's reference: anything C holds that reaches into zypy goes through a counted mirror, including items of C-born containers, so this root set is complete. A sweeper drops mirrors whose value is unmarked and whose count is the table's alone, and proxy entries whose box is unmarked; their C references are released in after-collection, where tp_dealloc may run C finalizers and program code. A proxy freed at a drop site releases at once through the box's dropper. Values that cross are passed through escaping parameters, so drop analysis never frees one C still sees. A cycle through C memory (an object-dtype ndarray holding a list that holds the array) is kept alive until the phase-6 cross-heap pass: while marking, a marked proxy's tp_traverse closure marks the mirrors it reaches, and C objects whose counts are explained by other tracked C objects (CPython's subtract_refs over the tracked set) are cleared with tp_clear in after-collection. The trace fixpoint in 842e0681 is the hook it runs in.\n\nHash agreement. numpy computes scalar hashes itself (np.int8(-1) hashes to -2, integer scalars through PyObject_Hash of a PyLong, floats through _Py_HashDouble), and those keys meet zypy's in zypy dicts. zypy's numeric hash therefore adopts CPython's definition: modulo 2^61-1, -1 maps to -2, and _Py_HashDouble for floats so hash(1) == hash(1.0) == hash(np.float64(1.0)). zypy's int hash is the value itself today. String hashes stay zypy's: the unicode tp_hash is zypy's string hash, and the plugin treats a str's hash field and any hash C passes (_PyDict_*_KnownHash, orjson's Py_HashBuffer writes) as untrusted, re-deriving it.\n\n\nTypes\n\nBuiltin types are exported static PyTypeObjects (the 31 in the cp314 union, and 35 PyExc_* pointers to static exception types), each a buffer sized for the largest row and filled at row selection: immortal count, tp_basicsize and itemsize from the row, the fast-subclass tp_flags bits every PyXxx_Check reads (LONG 1\u003c\u003c24 to TYPE 1\u003c\u003c31) plus READY, BASETYPE, IMMUTABLETYPE, HAVE_GC and HAVE_VECTORCALL as CPython sets them, and real slot tables. numpy calls PyUnicode_Type.tp_new and tp_dealloc, PyBytes_Type.tp_new, PyFloat_Type.tp_new, PyComplex_Type.tp_new and PyLong_Type.tp_as_number directly and embeds PyFloatObject, PyComplexObject, a whole PyUnicodeObject and PyBytesObject in its scalars, so these slots work over CPython layouts and handle subtypes as CPython's *_subtype_new do (str subtypes get the non-compact layout). PyType_Type.tp_basicsize is the row's sizeof(PyHeapTypeObject), which Cython's __Pyx_ImportType checks; bool and complex carry their real sizes.\n\nStatic types in extension data (about 40 in numpy) go through PyType_Ready: tp_base, tp_bases, tp_mro, tp_dict (a view over a zypy dict), CPython's inherit_special and inherit_slots rules, descriptors from tp_methods, tp_members and tp_getset with d_method, d_member and d_getset at 40 (numpy's add_docstring writes them), tp_version_tag, READY. Heap types from PyType_FromSpec, FromSpecWithBases, FromModuleAndSpec and FromMetaclass are allocated at the row's heap type size with the sub-tables at the row's offsets; ht_module feeds PyType_GetModuleByDef. pybind11 fills PyHeapTypeObject by hand before PyType_Ready, which the row layout supports. PyType_Modified bumps tp_version_tag. PyCFunctionObject (m_ml 16, m_self 24, m_module 32, vectorcall 48) and PyCMethodObject (64 bytes, embedded by Cython's CyFunction) have real layouts.\n\nA zypy class crossing into C gets a heap type, made once and cached by class index: tp_name and qualname, tp_mro from the zypy hierarchy, HEAPTYPE|BASETYPE, and only the slots whose dunders the class defines (so PyNumber_Check, PySequence_Check and PyIter_Check answer as CPython's would): tp_getattro and tp_setattro through py$getattr$dyn and setattr$dyn, tp_call and vectorcall through the keyword-aware call, tp_richcompare, tp_hash, tp_repr, tp_str, nb_*, mp_*, sq_contains, tp_iter and tp_iternext through the existing zb_hook_instance_* hooks. The prelude exception classes map one to one onto the static PyExc_* types; a user exception class gets a heap type whose tp_base chain ends at its PyExc_X, with BASE_EXC_SUBCLASS and a PyBaseExceptionObject body. Function values get a 'function' heap type with vectorcall and tp_descr_get; module values use PyModule_Type with md_dict a view over the module table.\n\nA Python class whose base is a C type (numpy.matrix, recarray and memmap on ndarray; record on np.void; markupsafe's Markup(str) and numpy's scalar subclasses on builtins) is a foreign-based class. Its methods compile as ordinary functions with self: Object; the class statement lowers to `$CPy$type_new(name, bases, namespace)`, whose PyType_Type.tp_new builds a heap type with tp_base the C type, tp_basicsize the base's plus a dict pointer and weaklist, tp_dictoffset set, tp_dict holding function mirrors, CPython-style slot_* wrappers for every defined dunder (so C sees `__array_finalize__` and `__getitem__` overrides), and __init_subclass__ and __set_name__ called. Instances are C objects held by proxies; their attributes live in the instance __dict__. This is the one place zypy runs a CPython object model, all of it in the plugin, and only for classes derived from C types.\n\nzypy using a C type: zb_hook_foreign_* call `$CPy$*`, which follow CPython's abstract protocol (binary_op1 with reflected operands, subtype priority and NotImplemented; GenericGetAttr with data descriptors, instance dict, non-data descriptors; mp_subscript then sq_item; tp_iternext with NULL-without-error as the end).\n\n\nCalls\n\nzypy to C: a call whose callee or receiver is FOREIGN reaches `$CPy$call0` to `call4`, `call_n`, `call_kw` or `call_method(recv, name_cell, site_cache, args...)`. Arguments go through to_c into a stack array with a spare slot 0 for PY_VECTORCALL_ARGUMENTS_OFFSET; the method name is a per-name static cell holding the interned PyUnicode after first use; kwnames tuples are real tuples of interned strings, built once per call site (Cython, PyO3 and pybind11 parse them themselves). PyObject_Vectorcall dispatches through tp_vectorcall_offset, else tp_call with a tuple and dict; PyCFunctions carry an adapter per METH_* form (NOARGS, O, VARARGS, VARARGS|KEYWORDS, FASTCALL, FASTCALL|KEYWORDS, METHOD|FASTCALL|KEYWORDS). A method call on a proxy uses _PyObject_GetMethod semantics and allocates no bound method; the site cache holds (type, tp_version_tag, descriptor, ml_meth, ml_flags) and a hit after an exact type compare calls ml_meth directly. Phase 5 lets compiled code make that call through an HIR IndirectCall, converting typed arguments straight from registers. NULL means the thread state holds an exception (Errors); otherwise the result goes through from_c.\n\nC to zypy: a mirror's vectorcall is a per-arity trampoline: from_c of each argument, keywords mapped through the record's parameter names (missing optionals pass $Host$missing_arg as dynamic calls do), the bound zb_call_N or the list form, then py$exc checked; a pending exception moves into the thread state and the trampoline returns NULL, otherwise to_c of the result as a new reference. PyObject_Call, CallObject, Vectorcall, VectorcallDict, VectorcallMethod and the Call* family reach the same path. The C and zypy frames share the native stack the collector already scans.\n\nPyInit_\u003cname\u003e (PyInitU_\u003cpunycode\u003e for non-ASCII), Py_mod_exec functions, tp_* slots, getters, setters and O\u0026 converters are called from Rust through transmuted `extern \"C\"` pointers; no compiler support is needed before the phase-5 caches. Py_EnterRecursiveCall checks the native stack pointer against the stack zypy runs programs on, raising RecursionError as 3.14's C stack check does.\n\n\nErrors\n\nThe error indicator lives where C reads it: a real PyThreadState per OS thread at the row's size, with interp at 16 pointing at one static PyInterpreterState. PyErr_* read and write only the row's error slot (current_exception, or the curexc triple normalized on demand), because Cython's FAST_THREAD_STATE reads and writes it inline. exc_info points at exc_state, a _PyErr_StackItem in the row's shape; when zypy code inside an except clause calls C, the boundary pushes the exception being handled, so sys.exc_info semantics hold in C. PyErr_SetString, SetObject and Format build C-born PyBaseExceptionObjects from a freelist and no zypy object, because Cython and numpy raise and clear AttributeError, KeyError and StopIteration constantly.\n\nC to zypy: a `$CPy$` entry that sees an error signal (NULL, -1, or -1 with PyErr_Occurred for the PyLong_As* family; SystemError \"error return without exception set\" if the slot is empty) calls the frontend's cold zb_hook_raise_foreign. A mirror's link gives back the original zypy exception. Otherwise the exception becomes an instance of the prelude class of its nearest builtin ancestor on tp_mro (one table built from the 35 PyExc_* statics), with str(exc) as the message and `$cpy` holding the C object, so `except ValueError` keeps its class-index range test. When the pending exception has `$cpy` set, the cold handler-dispatch test also asks PyErr_GivenExceptionMatches, so `except IndexError` on a C type with several builtin bases and `except yaml.YAMLError` against a class known only at run time both match. Re-raising or passing it into C hands over the original C object with its traceback. The pending check after the call is the ordinary one; `$CPy$` calls are fallible to the raise analysis.\n\nzypy to C: a trampoline, slot wrapper or hook that finishes with py$exc set stores the exception (its `$cpy` object, or its mirror) in the error slot, clears py$exc and returns NULL or -1 as the slot requires. StopIteration from a zypy __next__ called through tp_iternext becomes NULL with no error. py$exc stays one global: under the GIL it is empty at every release point.\n\nTracebacks: PyCode_NewEmpty, PyUnstable_Code_NewWithPosOnlyArgs, PyFrame_New (with the fields Cython writes inline at the row's offsets), PyTraceBack_Here and PyTraceBack_Print make small real objects; zypy's uncaught-exception report appends the C frames after its own. PyErr_WarnEx and WarnFormat print once per location to stderr as the default filters do; PyErr_WriteUnraisable prints as CPython does; PyErr_CheckSignals raises KeyboardInterrupt after SIGINT. C++ exceptions never cross the plugin: pybind11 and numpy's C++ catch them first, and trampolines abort with a message if a foreign unwind reaches them.\n\n\nLoading\n\nThe finder searches each package directory in CPython's order: `\u003cname\u003e/__init__.py`, `\u003cname\u003e.py`, then the row's suffixes (before a row is fixed, every row's `.cpython-3XY-\u003cplat\u003e.so`, newest first), `.abi3.so` (and `.abi3-\u003cmultiarch\u003e.so` on Linux), `.so`; Windows `.cp3XY-win_amd64.pyd` and `.pyd`. sys.path is the script directory, PYTHONPATH or ZYPY_PATH, then the site-packages of VIRTUAL_ENV or of a pyvenv.cfg next to the launcher. `$CPy$load_extension(fullname, path)`:\n1. On the first load only: the object layer for the row (static types, singletons, small ints, Py_Version, the main thread state, the GIL taken by the main thread, the SIGINT flag handler).\n2. zrtl_native Library::open(path, Local); symbol PyInit_\u003clast component\u003e.\n3. Package context set to the full name (single-phase PyModule_Create2 reads it, as _Py_PackageContext); PyInit called with the GIL held.\n4. A result whose ob_type is PyModuleDef_Type is multi-phase (25 of the 29 cp314 census modules): a ModuleSpec object (name, origin, parent, loader, submodule_search_locations, has_location, cached), Py_mod_create or PyModule_NewObject, md_def and md_state (m_size bytes), PyModule_AddFunctions, the Py_mod_exec slots in order; Py_mod_multiple_interpreters and Py_mod_gil are accepted. Otherwise it is single-phase (ujson, psutil, numpy _simd and test modules) and m_copy is kept for PyState_FindModule and reimport. From the 3.15 row, PyABIInfo is checked against the row.\n5. __file__, __spec__, __loader__ set; the module goes into sys.modules as a FOREIGN value and is bound in its parent package.\nNULL with an error set is the ImportError CPython would raise. Handles are never closed.\n\nPyImport_ImportModule, ImportModuleLevelObject, GetModule, AddModuleRef, GetModuleDict and PyCapsule_Import call the frontend's runtime import entry, which compiles and runs a Python module on demand (numpy._core._multiarray_umath's exec imports numpy._core._exceptions and others) and returns its module mirror. `import ext` and `from ext import a, b` bind Any slots filled when the statement runs. `from ext import *` needs the names when the importing module is compiled, so the extension loads then; with module-at-a-time compilation that is when its import runs, and the only visible difference from CPython is that PyInit runs before the importing module's earlier statements.\n\nExports: symbols.toml generates the list; zypy's build.rs hands it to export_link_args (macOS exported_symbols_list with `_main`, keeping -dead_strip_dylibs; Linux --dynamic-list, without which dlopen fails on an undefined symbol, checked in scratch). Gates: `nm -D zypy` on the NUC and `dyld_info -exports` on the Mac equal the list; `nm` on the capi rlib shows no `_zrtl_info` (the linked-into-host rule; macOS links a duplicate clean, the NUC does not). Windows extensions import from python3.dll and python3XY.dll by name, which an .exe cannot satisfy: code-free forwarder DLLs generated from the list, forwarding to zypy.exe, are the plan for phase 6, and whether they fit \"not a separate dylib\" is the user's call, as lua54.dll is for Lua.\n\n\nGIL and threads\n\nOne GIL (mutex and condition variable) in the plugin; the program thread takes it at the first extension load and holds it whenever Python code runs. PyEval_SaveThread, RestoreThread, AcquireThread and ReleaseThread (Py_BEGIN/END_ALLOW_THREADS: 13 uses in psutil, pervasive in numpy) release and reacquire it. PyGILState_Ensure and Release keep a per-thread counter mirrored into the row's gilstate_counter (pybind11 reads it inline) and create a thread state at the row layout for a foreign thread; PyGILState_Check, GetThisThreadState, PyThreadState_New, Clear, DeleteCurrent and Swap exist. PyInterpreterState_Get and Main return the one interpreter, GetID returns 0 (pydantic_core's subinterpreter guard passes), GetDict is a dict view (pybind11's internals). PyThread_tss_* map to pthread keys (TlsAlloc on Windows). PyMutex_Lock and Unlock (3.13+) work on numpy's one-byte statics with a compare-exchange fast path and an address-keyed parking lot. PyContextVar_* keep a per-thread context.\n\nUntil the phase-6 mutator handoff, the collector allows one mutator: a foreign thread that calls into zypy under PyGILState turns the collector off for the rest of the run (the program stays correct; ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC reports it). numpy's import and ufunc paths never do; pybind11 callbacks and threaded extensions do.\n\n\nBuffers, capsules, datetime\n\nPy_buffer (80 bytes) and PyBufferProcs are the same in every row. PyObject_GetBuffer, PyBuffer_Release, FillInfo, IsContiguous, ToContiguous, FromContiguous, GetPointer, SizeFromFormat, CheckBuffer; PyMemoryViewObject with the real layout (FromObject, FromMemory, FromBuffer, GetContiguous). zypy exporters: bytes (the mirror's ob_sval, read-only), bytearray (ob_start into the zypy buffer, with ob_exports refusing a resize), array.array (the typed list's storage with the typecode's format and itemsize). A resize while exported raises BufferError without a check on append: the export sets the list header's cap to its len and keeps the real cap and an export count in a side table, so the next append takes the grow branch, which is already out of line and consults the table. Typed lists export no buffer (a Python list does not). zypy consumes C buffers through memoryview, bytes(), array.frombytes and struct.unpack_from; a contiguous buffer of a matching format becomes a typed view pinned by its Py_buffer, which later gives zypy loops over numpy data unboxed element reads.\n\nPyCapsule is a real object (numpy's _ARRAY_API and _UFUNC_API pass between numpy's own modules and into numpy.random's Cython untouched). PyCapsule_Import(\"datetime.datetime_CAPI\") (numpy, orjson, pydantic_core) needs the 120-byte PyDateTime_CAPI and CPython-layout date, datetime, time, timedelta and tzinfo objects (PyDateTime_DateTime is 48 bytes with data at 25, read inline by the PyDateTime_GET_* macros). zypy's datetime is CPython's pure-Python _pydatetime compiled by zypy; the capsule's types are the heap types of its classes laid out as CPython lays them out, a mirror fills the data bytes once (the objects are immutable), and the capsule's constructors call into zypy. datetime semantics stay on the Python side.\n\n\nPrograms that import no extension pay nothing\n\n- No plugin state exists and no plugin code runs: every initialization happens at the first extension load. The `$CPy$*` symbols join the existing register_static_plugins batch (still one JIT rebuild), as the Lua design does.\n- Exports add no dyld work: a binary exporting all 513 cp314 names has the same 504 fixups as one exporting only `_main`, and is 45 KB larger (dyld_info on the scratch pair). Exported functions cannot be dead-stripped, so the plugin's code stays in the binary unpaged.\n- The FOREIGN arms in zyntax_builtins sit on TypeError paths; the frontend emits its own foreign arms only when an extension is reachable.\n- Typed fast paths are untouched: no representation changes, no new header words, no hash cost beyond the numeric-hash rule (a -1 check and a modulus that only values at or above 2^61-1 take).\n- Gate, as for Lua: release builds, main against branch, interleaved A/B/A/B, at least 300 launches per arm of an empty program and hello-world plus the speed-center rows, on the Mac and the NUC, p10/p50/p90 and peak RSS; medians must differ by less than the A/A spread. The Mac was at load 66 while this was designed, so nothing was timed.\n\nCost at the boundary: a METH_O or FASTCALL call with scalar arguments is a site-cache hit, freelist to_c, one indirect call, a NULL check and from_c; strings cost O(len) once per string. numpy's hot loops (ufunc inner loops, reductions, BLAS, random, FFT) stay in C; zypy crosses once per numpy call. A Python loop indexing an ndarray crosses per element, as CPython allocates a scalar per element. Targets, measured against CPython 3.14.7 and PyPy 3.11 (its cpyext with pp311 wheels) on both machines: per-crossing cost within 1.2x of CPython, whole programs ahead of both.\n\n\nPhases\n\nPhase 0, generic pieces (5 days; shared with c17ca09391383182a1cf10565b3676b5eddfa2325184d78ecc9e30515476f489, whichever lands first builds them): sdk/zrtl_native Library and export_link_args with the three build.rs files moved onto it; 842e0681 merged to main; FOREIGN_TAG and the foreign hooks in zyntax_builtins behind Policy.foreign_hooks; the indirect-call convention test on all three tiers. Proof: a test cdylib that binds back to a symbol its test executable exports, on the Mac and the NUC; `nm -D`/`dyld_info -exports` equal `_main` plus the list; conformance and the pressure test unchanged with the hooks compiled in.\n\nPhase 1, 3.14 GIL: hand-written C and abi3 (20 days). markupsafe._speedups (2 symbols), ujson (64, single-phase), simplejson._speedups (89: PyUnicodeWriter_*, heap types with module state), psutil (cp36-abi3, 40, 64-bit ++/-- refcounts, PyType_GetFlags): 137 symbols. Delivers the capi crate skeleton (symbols.toml, generated exports, the full stub set, varargs.c), the 3.14 row and the row checker, pre-header allocator, immortals, MIRRORS and PROXIES with the collector hooks, to_c and from_c, the builtin types with real slots for int, float, complex, bool, str, bytes, bytearray, tuple, list, dict and set, list and dict views, module objects and both init forms, PyCFunction with every METH_* form, PyArg and Py_BuildValue, errors and the thread state, the GIL with SaveThread and RestoreThread, bytes and bytearray buffers, the loader; frontend: module values, sys.modules, packages and relative imports (enough for these four packages), the finder, FOREIGN arms, keyword calls on the dynamic path, the `$cpy` field, and foreign-based classes on builtins for Markup(str). Proof: (a) a differential probe, a C extension built at test time against the vendored 3.14 headers that exercises every phase-1 function on values passed from Python, run under CPython 3.14.7 (Mac, uv venv) and 3.14.4 (NUC) and under zypy, outputs diffed; (b) a cext conformance category with CPython 3.14.7's output: markupsafe _escape_inner over ASCII, latin-1, UCS2 and UCS4 plus the identity case, then the whole markupsafe package; ujson and simplejson round trips including their error texts and simplejson's object_hook calling back into zypy; psutil functions; (c) pressure/cext_crossings: 200,000 ujson round trips of lists, strings, dicts and instances with bounded RSS; (d) the suite under ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1; (e) ZYPY_CAPI_AUDIT=1 shows no growth of live C-API objects between 2k and 200k iterations.\n\nPhase 2, 3.14 GIL: Cython tier and PyO3 abi3 (15 days). pyyaml._yaml (146) and msgpack._cmsgpack (190), 258 cumulative, then cryptography's cp311-abi3 PyO3 module and the numpy.random Cython modules (251 alone) as the bridge to numpy. Delivers the full thread state Cython reads inline (current_exception 112, exc_info 120, exc_state), PyType_Ready over static types, FromSpec and FromMetaclass heap types, _PyType_Lookup with the version cache, every tp_* slot callable directly, lv_tag digit access, in-place list append through the list view, descriptors, PyCMethodObject for CyFunction, vectorcall on every path, VectorcallMethod and VectorcallDict, PyCode, PyFrame and PyTraceBack, the GC API (GC_New, Track, UnTrack, Del, IsFinalized, CallFinalizerFromDealloc, managed-dict Clear and Visit), capsules, memoryview, PyFloat_Pack and Unpack, Py_Version, _PyDict_GetItem_KnownHash, _PyDict_NewPresized, _PyObject_GC_New, _PyObject_GetDictPtr, _PyByteArray_empty_string, PyUnstable_Object_EnableDeferredRefcount (returns 0), __Pyx_ImportType's size checks passing; for PyO3 limited mode, _Py_IncRef and _Py_DecRef, PyModule_FromDefAndSpec2 and ExecDef, 27 PyInit_ entries in one file, Py_GenericAlias. Proof: a Cython 3.3.0 probe (.pyx exercising each CYTHON_USE_* internal) diffed against CPython 3.14.7; msgpack round trips of every type, Unpacker streaming, ExtType, Timestamp, bytearray and memoryview inputs; msgpack's ExtraData and FormatError raised from C and caught by `except ValueError` with CPython's traceback lines; yaml CLoader and CDumper over the PyYAML test data files; cryptography hashes.Hash(SHA256) matching hashlib.\n\nPhase 3, older rows in descending order, abi3 alongside (10 days). 3.13 (current_exception 112, gilstate 136, tp_versions_used; its names _PyLong_Sign, _Py_HashBytes, the KnownHash_LockHeld alias). 3.12 (tuple ob_item 24, current_exception 96, gilstate 120, the _SizeT names, borrowed PyImport_AddModule, _PyObject_LookupAttr, _PyObject_GenericGetAttrWithDict, PyGILState_Check). 3.11, the first semantic switches, written once and keyed by the row's enums: SignInSize longs, unicode with wstr (48 and 72) and ready=1 plus _PyUnicode_Ready, the curexc triple at 96, Cython's dict version tags bumped on mutation, exc_info 120, Py_OptimizeFlag. 3.10 (curexc at 88, exc_info 144, gilstate 160, the four-word exc item). 3.9 (24-byte PyAsyncMethods shifting the heap-type offsets, _PyGen_Send, _Py_NewReference, _PyFloat_Pack*, PyCoro_Type and PyGen_Type). Proof per row: the row checker against that version's headers; the phase 1 and 2 cext programs and probes re-run with that tag's wheels against uv's CPython of that minor version on the Mac and on the NUC (with abi3 psutil and cryptography under every row); the scratch hosts already load markupsafe for cp314, cp313, cp312, cp311 and cp39 with one implementation.\n\nPhase 4, numpy 2.5.3 cp314 (20 days of C-API work, gated by the prerequisites below). _multiarray_umath (319 symbols) and _umath_linalg, then all 23 modules (439 cumulative). Delivers the full PyNumber_* set, PyComplex_*, PySlice_*, PySequence_* and PyMapping_*, PyDictProxy, PyMem_Raw*, PyTraceMalloc_Track and Untrack, the descriptor types, PyUnicode_AsUCS4, Compare, Tailmatch and Replace, the 8 _PyUnicode_Is* functions from Rust's Unicode tables, _Py_HashDouble, _Py_ascii_whitespace, _PyObject_New and NewVar, PyContextVar_*, PyGILState_*, PyMutex, PyInterpreterState_Main, the PyUnstable_Object_* functions, PyLong_IsZero, C-level subclassing of float, complex, str, bytes and int with numpy's scalars readied on them, foreign-based classes on ndarray and np.void, the datetime capsule over _pydatetime. Proof: cext programs compared with CPython 3.14.7 (import, creation, dtypes, broadcasting, reductions, fancy indexing, printing, isinstance(np.float64(1.5), float) and hash equality, np.str_ as str, linalg solve/inv/det through OpenBLAS, fft, default_rng(42) and RandomState(0) streams identical, datetime64, np.matrix and np.memmap, object arrays holding zypy values); then numpy's _core tests (test_multiarray, test_umath, test_ufunc, test_numeric, test_scalarmath, test_dtype, test_indexing) through a pytest-free runner, compared test by test against CPython 3.14.4 on the NUC; `import numpy` time and array benchmarks against CPython and PyPy. The same programs then re-run under the 3.13 to 3.9 rows with each tag's numpy.\n\nPhase 5, pybind11, PyO3 non-abi3, orjson, and inline caches (12 days, 513 cumulative). pybind11 (contourpy, +27): hand-filled heap types through PyType_Ready, PyThread_tss, PyInterpreterState_GetDict, PyInstanceMethod, PyCMethod_New, PyFrame_GetCode, GetBack and GetLineNumber, thread-state lifecycle. PyO3 (pydantic_core) and pyo3-ffi (orjson), +47: PyLongWriter_*, PyLong_Export, FreeExport and GetNativeLayout, PyUnicode_DATA as a function, PyType_Freeze, GetName and GetQualName, _PyDict_SetItem_KnownHash_LockHeld, Py_HashBuffer, _PyLong_AsByteArray, and orjson's inline ob_refcnt decrements and utf8 reads. Per-site inline caches that call ml_meth from compiled code through IndirectCall with typed argument conversion; the dying-temporary flag for IsUniqueReferencedTemporary so numpy's `a + b + c` reuses buffers. Proof: contourpy lines, pydantic_core validation, orjson round trips of datetimes and numpy scalars, compared with CPython 3.14.7; the boundary benchmarks (ext_call_noargs, markupsafe escape of 100 characters, ujson and msgpack small documents, numpy add on 10 and 1e7 elements, a per-element numpy loop) interleaved against CPython 3.14.7 and PyPy 3.11 on the Mac and the NUC.\n\nPhase 6, hardening (15 days). The cross-heap cycle pass on the 842e0681 trace fixpoint; the generic mutator handoff so foreign threads may allocate and call zypy with the collector on; warning filters; Windows (the .def export, forwarder DLLs if the user accepts them, the fixture build on the Windows CI leg). Proof: pressure/cext_cycles (a zypy object holding an object-dtype ndarray that holds it, 200k iterations, bounded); a C thread calling back into zypy under PyGILState with ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1; Windows CI green.\n\nPhase 7, free-threaded builds, last (30 days or more, most of it runtime work). cp314t then cp313t (numpy, msgpack, pydantic_core and markupsafe publish them) are a second table family, not a row: a 32-byte header {ob_tid, ob_flags u16, ob_mutex, ob_gc_bits, ob_ref_local u32, ob_ref_shared, ob_type at 24}, so every body offset moves by 16; immortal is ob_ref_local == UINT32_MAX; ob_tid must equal what the inlined _Py_ThreadId reads (the TLS base register: tpidrro_el0 on macOS arm64, fs base on x86_64 Linux); biased refcount merge and dealloc (_Py_DecRefShared, _Py_MergeZeroLocalRefcount, _Py_SetImmortal; _Py_Dealloc is no longer imported); PyCriticalSection_Begin and End on ob_mutex; PyList_GetItemRef, PyMapping_GetOptionalItem, PyUnstable_Module_SetGIL; the aligned unicode state from 3.15t. Without a GIL zypy itself must run Python on several threads at once: a multi-mutator collector with safepoints, a thread-safe pool, a thread-local py$exc, and thread-safe container mutation or a critical section around every zypy operation on a crossed value. Proof: the phase 1, 2 and 4 programs with the cp314t wheels against CPython 3.14t, plus a threaded stress run under ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1.\n\n\nPrerequisites (frontend and stdlib work, tracked on their own issues, larger than the C API for numpy)\n\nFor phase 1: module objects as values and sys.modules; packages with __init__.py and relative imports (ce52e09248028b277aa63fd1f530019534fa3e3e00b888bd8faf433fc06e2dd9); a runtime import entry C can call; try/except ImportError around imports (91966345584f98f76b8e78af37884ef4d40ea3548b4cda14d1cda2b37402c490); keyword calls on the dynamic call path (function records carry parameter names); a zypy class subclassing str (markupsafe's Markup).\n\nFor phase 2: class decorators, multiple inheritance of builtin exceptions, and what pyyaml's and msgpack's Python layers use.\n\nFor numpy (phase 4), from the census of what `import numpy` executes on CPython 3.14: about 60 stdlib modules before user code and 143 more modules on import, 82 numpy .py modules (about 59,700 lines). zypy stops today at `import numpy` (\"import of module `numpy`, which was not found\"); probing numpy's submodules one at a time stops at enum, types, collections, numbers, builtins, re, ast and contextvars not found, relative imports, class decorators, \"more than one base class\" (AxisError(ValueError, IndexError)), and \"calling through a value\" at run time. Needed:\n1. A runtime import system: module-at-a-time compilation at the import statement, packages, relative imports (124 in 33 modules), `from m import *` including from C modules (17), circular partial imports (numpy, numpy._core, numpy.lib), sys.modules, module-level __getattr__ and __dir__, __all__ built with +=, __import__ and globals(), sys._getframe.\n2. The stdlib the chain touches (49a1570ce42e172eaa1224a7bfb0f48a6bd78a7cd92cd81e0ca29c023f30a05e): enum, types, collections, numbers, builtins, re, ast, contextvars, contextlib, inspect, functools, operator, warnings, weakref, pickle, struct, math, platform, sysconfig, textwrap, threading, typing, datetime (as _pydatetime, which the capsule also needs), and ctypes optionally (numpy guards it). Several of these sit on CPython C accelerators built into libpython (_sre, _ast, _collections, _functools, _operator, _contextvars, _typing, _tokenize), which cannot be loaded as extensions; zypy provides them natively.\n3. Language: 374 function and 34 class decorators (numpy's set_module), keyword-only and positional-only parameters, *args and **kwargs, generators, lambda, with, del, f-strings, walrus, match, `type X = ...` aliases (112 in numpy._typing), multiple inheritance of builtin exceptions, __slots__, __new__, __init_subclass__, __getattribute__, super(), class body statements (262d151f9d577e77d3ea67ebe84943179abce3d0cd98968885ddab9566e4fd5e), and isinstance, type, getattr and hasattr on objects from C.\n4. The numeric hash rule above (CPython's int and float hashes), which numpy scalar keys need in zypy dicts.\n5. Arbitrary-precision int (d692f0a354b274e582b56404f376a4777693f629e73fee7f3a100e3b83c19c02), so PyLongs beyond i64 convert instead of staying proxies.\n6. A per-module compile cache (per-module HIR snapshots), or 59,700 lines of numpy compile on every start and zypy's start-up advantage is gone.\n\n\nRisks\n\n- Lists retained by C are synced at every transition in O(n) per retained list, and a retained C-born list stays a proxy without typed-list speed. PyList_SET_ITEM on a retained mirror is invisible to zypy until the next return from C. ZYPY_CAPI_TRACE=1 reports the retained set.\n- Cycles through C memory leak until phase 6.\n- A callback from a C-created thread turns the collector off until phase 6.\n- Foreign-based classes need CPython-accurate type_new, slot_* wrappers, MRO and __dict__ handling in the plugin; it is the part of numpy's surface (matrix, recarray, memmap) most likely to find differences.\n- Refcount-visible behaviour: zypy aliases count as one reference, so ndarray.resize succeeds where CPython refuses; extensions asserting exact refcounts in their own tests see other numbers.\n- Private and unstable symbols (_PyType_Lookup, _PyDict_GetItem_KnownHash, _PyObject_GetDictPtr, _PyObject_GC_New, _PyUnicode_Is*, PyUnstable_*) have no contract and change between releases; each new CPython release needs a census diff of published wheels besides its row, and the differential probes are the check.\n- A release that changes what inline macros do (3.12's immortals, 3.14's tuple ob_hash) needs code, not only a row; the row checker and the probes catch it against the new headers.\n- Semantics that differ from CPython: ints beyond i64 before bigint; shaped tuples are new PyTuples each crossing; string hash values are zypy's; PyInit of a star-imported extension runs when the importing module is compiled.\n- Row per process: a venv mixing cp313 and cp314 wheels cannot load both (CPython cannot either); the error names both tags.\n- Building zypy needs a C compiler through cc (as for Lua); running it does not.\n- Free-threaded support is a thread-safe zypy runtime, not a table.\n\n\nDecisions for the user\n\n1. Windows: extensions import python3.dll and python3XY.dll by name. Serving prebuilt Windows wheels needs code-free forwarder DLLs to zypy.exe next to the executable; they carry no API code but are DLLs. Same question as lua54.dll in the Lua design.\n2. Vendoring CPython's Include/ trees (PSF-2.0, 3.9 to 3.14, later 3.15) into the capi crate for the row checker, the probes and the fixture extensions. The implementation itself is written fresh from the documented API and header layouts; porting CPython C sources (getargs.c, typeobject.c's slot wrappers) would bring PSF-licensed code into the repository.\n3. Where zypy finds installed packages: reuse a CPython venv's site-packages that pip filled (VIRTUAL_ENV, pyvenv.cfg, ZYPY_PATH), which this design assumes, or give zypy its own installer.\n4. The stdlib strategy for numpy's import chain: compile CPython's pure-Python Lib sources with zypy (PSF-2.0, with zypy-native replacements for the accelerators built into libpython) or write zypy equivalents module by module.\n5. Whether free-threaded wheels are worth phase 7, given that they require multi-threaded Python execution in zypy's runtime, not only a second header table.","files":null}]}